Corresponding source for the LGPL components
--------------------------------------------

FFmpeg (https://ffmpeg.org), used unmodified as the ffmpeg and ffprobe
executables, under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or later.
The bundled build is configured with --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree
--disable-autodetect, so it contains no GPL-licensed or non-free components and
links no external libraries. scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh reproduces it exactly from
the upstream release it pins. As the LGPL requires, the corresponding source is
published alongside each release as
Musix-<version>-ffmpeg-<ffmpeg version>-source.tar.xz, which is the unmodified
tarball the bundled binaries were built from.

glib and gettext are LGPL-2.1-or-later. Section 6 of that license offers no
equivalent of GPLv3 section 6(d), which is what permits a third-party server;
its option 6(d) requires offering the source "from the same place". Their
corresponding source is therefore published alongside each release too, as
Musix-<version>-glib-<version>-source.tar.xz and
Musix-<version>-gettext-<version>-source.tar.xz. scripts/fetch-lgpl-sources.sh
builds them, pinned by checksum.

Homebrew builds glib from a patched tree, so upstream alone would not be its
corresponding source; the glib archive contains the upstream release, the
Homebrew patch and the formula that supplies the build arguments, all from
Homebrew/homebrew-core revision
fad6cdb45503e4976aeb7b1c4c4e0ec972f66013 -- the revision whose formula
declares the bottle digest recorded in the installed keg's sbom.spdx.json.
Homebrew applies no patch to gettext, so that archive is the upstream release
and the formula.

Qt 6.11.2 is LGPL-3.0-only, which incorporates GPLv3, and GPLv3 section 6(d)
permits conveying the Corresponding Source from a different server provided
there are clear directions to it. These are those directions. The complete
corresponding source for the bundled Qt is the unmodified upstream release:

  https://download.qt.io/archive/qt/6.11/6.11.2/single/qt-everywhere-src-6.11.2.tar.xz

Homebrew's qt formula applies no patches, so that release is the source the
bundled frameworks were built from. Qt is dynamically linked as separate
frameworks inside Musix.app/Contents/Frameworks and can be replaced with a
modified build, and Musix's own source is published under the MIT License at
https://github.com/ImanMontajabi/Musix.

CPython (https://python.org), redistributed as a relocatable build from
python-build-standalone (https://github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone),
under the Python Software Foundation License.

yt-dlp (Unlicense) and ytmusicapi (MIT) are installed into that runtime, and
the application updates those two packages in place.

No fonts are bundled. Musix uses an installed Google Sans Flex or Noto Sans font.
Musix is independent of Google and YouTube.
